LAZ test fixtures for the specular-points LAS/LAZ reader
=========================================================

  p1.las  p1.laz    LAS 1.2, point format 1  (GPS time)
  p3.las  p3.laz    LAS 1.2, point format 3  (GPS time, RGB)
  p6.las  p6.laz    LAS 1.4, point format 6  (extended returns, scanner channel, GPS time)
  p7.las  p7.laz    LAS 1.4, point format 7  (format 6 plus RGB)
  p8.las  p8.laz    LAS 1.4, point format 8  (format 7 plus NIR)

Each pair holds the same 2500 points. The .laz is LASzip-compressed (point-wise for formats
1 and 3, layered for 6, 7 and 8), and the .las is its uncompressed twin. The tests in
src/tests.rs check two things:

  laz_matches_its_las   the LAZ decoder gives exactly the points the LAS reader gives
  robust_laz            damaged copies of the .laz files fail without panicking

The data is synthetic. It is a made-up strip scan: 7 scan lines over rolling terrain at
UTM-like coordinates. Intensity, returns, classes, scan angle, user data, point source
IDs, GPS time jumps, scanner channels, RGB and NIR all vary, so every LASzip item is
exercised. The points do not come from any survey, so there is no licence to track.

The files were written by laspy 2.7.0 with the lazrs backend. To regenerate them, run
from this directory:

  PYTHON -I ../../gen/make_laz_fixtures.py        (needs laspy[lazrs] and numpy)

The script uses a fixed seed. It writes the same formats, fields and value ranges as the
committed files, but it was rewritten after them, so its random values differ from theirs.
That does not matter: the tests only compare each .laz with its own .las.
